{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0179pkeCdHgEgVQZZUpuxkMf","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\"\"\"\n     2→Cross-chapter character consensus builder.\n     3→\n     4→Merges character proposals across chapters, resolves aliases,\n     5→and produces the final CharacterMap.\n     6→\"\"\"\n     7→\n     8→import re\n     9→import hashlib\n    10→import time\n    11→from typing import Optional, Iterable\n    12→from collections import defaultdict\n    13→from difflib import SequenceMatcher\n    14→import logging\n    15→\n    16→from .models import (\n    17→    CharacterProposal,\n    18→    CharacterValidationResult,\n    19→    Character,\n    20→    CharacterMap,\n    21→    CharacterMention,\n    22→    CharacterType,\n    23→)\n    24→from .constants import are_potential_nicknames, get_nickname_variants\n    25→from ..llm import LLMClient\n    26→\n    27→logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n    28→\n    29→# Character classification prompts\n    30→CHARACTER_TYPE_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst classifying character types.\n    31→\n    32→Classify each character as one of:\n    33→- STORY: An active participant in the narrative who appears in scenes, speaks dialogue, or takes actions\n    34→- HISTORICAL: A real historical figure mentioned in passing (e.g., Napoleon, Einstein)\n    35→- REFERENCED: A fictional character from other works mentioned in passing (e.g., Hamlet, Sherlock Holmes)\n    36→\n    37→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n    38→\n    39→CHARACTER_TYPE_PROMPT = \"\"\"Classify this character's role in the narrative.\n    40→\n    41→CHARACTER: {name}\n    42→MENTION COUNT: {mention_count}\n    43→CHAPTERS PRESENT: {chapters}\n    44→SAMPLE CONTEXTS:\n    45→{contexts}\n    46→\n    47→Based on the contexts, determine if this character:\n    48→1. Actively participates (speaks, acts, appears in scenes) → STORY\n    49→2. Is a real historical figure mentioned in passing → HISTORICAL\n    50→3. Is a fictional character from other works → REFERENCED\n    51→\n    52→Return JSON:\n    53→{{\n    54→  \"character_type\": \"story\" | \"historical\" | \"referenced\",\n    55→  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n    56→  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n    57→}}\"\"\"\n    58→\n    59→# Common titles that should be ignored when comparing character names\n    60→# These are not part of the actual name but indicate social status or role\n    61→TITLES = {'mr', 'mrs', 'ms', 'miss', 'dr', 'sir', 'lady', 'lord', 'professor', 'captain', 'major'}\n    62→\n    63→# Epithet alias resolution prompt\n    64→EPITHET_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether different descriptive handles (epithets) refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n    65→\n    66→Descriptive handles are phrases like \"the creature\", \"the monster\", \"the old man\", \"the detective\".\n    67→\n    68→CRITICAL RULES:\n    69→1. Only merge epithets that CLEARLY refer to the same entity with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n    70→2. Use chapter appearances and context clues to verify they're the same character\n    71→3. Different descriptive characters (e.g., \"the old man\" in cottage vs \"the old man\" at inn) should NOT be merged\n    72→4. Generic phrases that could refer to anyone should NOT be merged together\n    73→\n    74→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n    75→\n    76→EPITHET_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n    77→\n    78→DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES (with chapter info and sample contexts):\n    79→{epithets}\n    80→\n    81→For each group of handles that refer to the SAME entity:\n    82→1. Pick the most specific/unique handle as canonical (e.g., \"the creature\" over \"the monster\")\n    83→2. List all other handles as aliases\n    84→\n    85→Return JSON array:\n    86→```json\n    87→[\n    88→  {{\"canonical\": \"the creature\", \"aliases\": [\"the monster\", \"the wretch\", \"the fiend\"]}},\n    89→  {{\"canonical\": \"the old man\", \"aliases\": []}}\n    90→]\n    91→```\n    92→\n    93→Return ONLY the JSON array. Include only handles you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n    94→\n    95→# Cross-group (epithet-to-proper-name) resolution prompt\n    96→CROSS_GROUP_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether descriptive handles (epithets) refer to named characters.\n    97→\n    98→Examples:\n    99→- \"the prince\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\"\n   100→- \"the duke\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\" (if the story mentions he's also a duke)\n   101→- \"the creature\" might refer to \"Adam\" (if named)\n   102→- \"my father\" might refer to \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n   103→\n   104→CRITICAL RULES:\n   105→1. Only link when context CLEARLY indicates they're the same person\n   106→2. Use chapter co-appearances and context clues\n   107→3. Titles/roles (the king, the general, my father) can refer to properly-named characters\n   108→4. Generic epithets that could be anyone should NOT be linked\n   109→5. DO NOT link if the epithet and proper name are in CONFLICT, OPPOSITION, or CONFRONTATION\n   110→   - If context shows them interacting as separate entities, they are different people\n   111→   - Examples: \"the intruder\" confronting \"Prince Prospero\", \"the stranger\" fighting \"the hero\"\n   112→6. DO NOT link if one entity DIES in interaction with the other entity\n   113→   - If context shows one character dying near/after encountering the other, they are DIFFERENT people\n   114→   - Examples: \"fell prostrate in death\" near another entity, \"killed by\" another entity\n   115→   - Death in proximity to another character means they cannot be the same person\n   116→\n   117→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n   118→\n   119→CROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\n   120→\n   121→DESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\n   122→{epithets}\n   123→\n   124→NAMED CHARACTERS:\n   125→{proper_names}\n   126→\n   127→IMPORTANT: Read the context snippets carefully. If an epithet and proper name appear in contexts showing them as SEPARATE ENTITIES (confronting, fighting, observing each other), DO NOT link them.\n   128→\n   129→For each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\n   130→\n   131→Return JSON array:\n   132→```json\n   133→[\n   134→  {{\"epithet\": \"the prince\", \"proper_name\": \"Prince Prospero\"}},\n   135→  {{\"epithet\": \"my father\", \"proper_name\": \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"}}\n   136→]\n   137→```\n   138→\n   139→Return ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n   140→\n   141→PAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n   142→\n   143→CRITICAL:\n   144→- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\n   145→- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\n   146→- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.\n   147→\n   148→Return ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n   149→\n   150→PAIRWISE_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Decide whether these two names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\n   151→\n   152→NAME A: {name_a}\n   153→MENTIONS A: {mentions_a}\n   154→CHAPTERS A: {chapters_a}\n   155→CONTEXT A:\n   156→{contexts_a}\n   157→\n   158→NAME B: {name_b}\n   159→MENTIONS B: {mentions_b}\n   160→CHAPTERS B: {chapters_b}\n   161→CONTEXT B:\n   162→{contexts_b}\n   163→\n   164→Rules:\n   165→- Only say same_person=true if there is strong evidence they are the same character (name overlap, title variants, obvious nickname/full-name relationship, or clear contextual cues).\n   166→- If the only overlap is a last name, be VERY cautious (family members/spouses share last names).\n   167→- If they never appear in overlapping chapters, be cautious unless it's clearly a full-name/short-name relationship.\n   168→\n   169→Return JSON:\n   170→{{\n   171→  \"same_person\": true/false,\n   172→  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n   173→  \"canonical\": \"Which of the two names should be canonical if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\",\n   174→  \"alias\": \"The other name if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\",\n   175→  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n   176→}}\"\"\"\n   177→\n   178→\n   179→ALIAS_RESOLUTION_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying character aliases.\n   180→\n   181→Your task is to determine which character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n   182→\n   183→IMPORTANT: Titles, ranks, and honorifics should be IGNORED when matching names.\n   184→The underlying PERSON is what matters, not how they're addressed. Examples:\n   185→- Military: \"SSgt Otto\" = \"Staff Sergeant Mark Otto\" = \"Mark Otto\" (but NOT just \"Otto\" alone)\n   186→- Civilian: \"Mr. Smith\" = \"John Smith\" (but NOT just \"Smith\" alone - could be family member)\n   187→- Religious: \"Father O'Brien\" = \"Patrick O'Brien\" (but NOT just \"O'Brien\" alone)\n   188→- Academic: \"Professor Williams\" = \"Dr. Williams\" = \"Jane Williams\"\n   189→\n   190→CRITICAL RULES - BE CONSERVATIVE:\n   191→1. Only merge names if they CLEARLY refer to the same individual with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n   192→2. Different people with similar names should NEVER be merged\n   193→3. Pay close attention to chapter appearances and context clues\n   194→4. When in doubt, keep names SEPARATE - false negatives are better than false positives\n   195→5. A bare FIRST name can merge with a full name (e.g., \"Mike\" → \"Michael Anderson\")\n   196→6. A bare LAST name is RISKY - only merge if you're absolutely certain (could be family member)\n   197→\n   198→For the canonical name, prefer the FULL CIVILIAN NAME (first + last) over titled versions.\n   199→\n   200→NEVER merge:\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"510b9767-e567-40b5-bc06-3d57a25e9010","uuid":"a5139dab-1942-4eed-b9fd-70dab851baca","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/zacharymandrews/Tools/audiobook_agent/src/pipeline/character_extraction/consensus.py","content":"\"\"\"\nCross-chapter character consensus builder.\n\nMerges character proposals across chapters, resolves aliases,\nand produces the final CharacterMap.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport re\nimport hashlib\nimport time\nfrom typing import Optional, Iterable\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom difflib import SequenceMatcher\nimport logging\n\nfrom .models import (\n    CharacterProposal,\n    CharacterValidationResult,\n    Character,\n    CharacterMap,\n    CharacterMention,\n    CharacterType,\n)\nfrom .constants import are_potential_nicknames, get_nickname_variants\nfrom ..llm import LLMClient\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n# Character classification prompts\nCHARACTER_TYPE_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst classifying character types.\n\nClassify each character as one of:\n- STORY: An active participant in the narrative who appears in scenes, speaks dialogue, or takes actions\n- HISTORICAL: A real historical figure mentioned in passing (e.g., Napoleon, Einstein)\n- REFERENCED: A fictional character from other works mentioned in passing (e.g., Hamlet, Sherlock Holmes)\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nCHARACTER_TYPE_PROMPT = \"\"\"Classify this character's role in the narrative.\n\nCHARACTER: {name}\nMENTION COUNT: {mention_count}\nCHAPTERS PRESENT: {chapters}\nSAMPLE CONTEXTS:\n{contexts}\n\nBased on the contexts, determine if this character:\n1. Actively participates (speaks, acts, appears in scenes) → STORY\n2. Is a real historical figure mentioned in passing → HISTORICAL\n3. Is a fictional character from other works → REFERENCED\n\nReturn JSON:\n{{\n  \"character_type\": \"story\" | \"historical\" | \"referenced\",\n  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n}}\"\"\"\n\n# Common titles that should be ignored when comparing character names\n# These are not part of the actual name but indicate social status or role\nTITLES = {'mr', 'mrs', 'ms', 'miss', 'dr', 'sir', 'lady', 'lord', 'professor', 'captain', 'major'}\n\n# Epithet alias resolution prompt\nEPITHET_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether different descriptive handles (epithets) refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n\nDescriptive handles are phrases like \"the creature\", \"the monster\", \"the old man\", \"the detective\".\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n1. Only merge epithets that CLEARLY refer to the same entity with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n2. Use chapter appearances and context clues to verify they're the same character\n3. Different descriptive characters (e.g., \"the old man\" in cottage vs \"the old man\" at inn) should NOT be merged\n4. Generic phrases that could refer to anyone should NOT be merged together\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nEPITHET_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to the SAME unnamed character.\n\nDESCRIPTIVE HANDLES (with chapter info and sample contexts):\n{epithets}\n\nFor each group of handles that refer to the SAME entity:\n1. Pick the most specific/unique handle as canonical (e.g., \"the creature\" over \"the monster\")\n2. List all other handles as aliases\n\nReturn JSON array:\n```json\n[\n  {{\"canonical\": \"the creature\", \"aliases\": [\"the monster\", \"the wretch\", \"the fiend\"]}},\n  {{\"canonical\": \"the old man\", \"aliases\": []}}\n]\n```\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array. Include only handles you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n\n# Cross-group (epithet-to-proper-name) resolution prompt\nCROSS_GROUP_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether descriptive handles (epithets) refer to named characters.\n\nExamples:\n- \"the prince\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\"\n- \"the duke\" might refer to \"Prince Prospero\" (if the story mentions he's also a duke)\n- \"the creature\" might refer to \"Adam\" (if named)\n- \"my father\" might refer to \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"\n\nCRITICAL RULES:\n1. Only link when context CLEARLY indicates they're the same person\n2. Use chapter co-appearances and context clues\n3. Titles/roles (the king, the general, my father) can refer to properly-named characters\n4. Generic epithets that could be anyone should NOT be linked\n5. DO NOT link if the epithet and proper name are in CONFLICT, OPPOSITION, or CONFRONTATION\n   - If context shows them interacting as separate entities, they are different people\n   - Examples: \"the intruder\" confronting \"Prince Prospero\", \"the stranger\" fighting \"the hero\"\n6. DO NOT link if one entity DIES in interaction with the other entity\n   - If context shows one character dying near/after encountering the other, they are DIFFERENT people\n   - Examples: \"fell prostrate in death\" near another entity, \"killed by\" another entity\n   - Death in proximity to another character means they cannot be the same person\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nCROSS_GROUP_PROMPT = \"\"\"Identify which descriptive handles refer to properly-named characters.\n\nDESCRIPTIVE HANDLES:\n{epithets}\n\nNAMED CHARACTERS:\n{proper_names}\n\nIMPORTANT: Read the context snippets carefully. If an epithet and proper name appear in contexts showing them as SEPARATE ENTITIES (confronting, fighting, observing each other), DO NOT link them.\n\nFor each epithet that CLEARLY refers to a named character, return a match.\n\nReturn JSON array:\n```json\n[\n  {{\"epithet\": \"the prince\", \"proper_name\": \"Prince Prospero\"}},\n  {{\"epithet\": \"my father\", \"proper_name\": \"Alphonse Frankenstein\"}}\n]\n```\n\nReturn ONLY the JSON array. Include only matches you're confident about - omit uncertain ones.\"\"\"\n\nPAIRWISE_ALIAS_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying whether two character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n\nCRITICAL:\n- Use ONLY the evidence provided below (chapter appearances and context snippets).\n- Be conservative: false negatives (missing an alias) are better than false positives (merging different people).\n- Titles (Mr/Mrs/Miss/Dr/etc.) are not part of the name; treat them as address forms.\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON. No other text.\"\"\"\n\nPAIRWISE_ALIAS_PROMPT = \"\"\"Decide whether these two names refer to the SAME PERSON in this novel.\n\nNAME A: {name_a}\nMENTIONS A: {mentions_a}\nCHAPTERS A: {chapters_a}\nCONTEXT A:\n{contexts_a}\n\nNAME B: {name_b}\nMENTIONS B: {mentions_b}\nCHAPTERS B: {chapters_b}\nCONTEXT B:\n{contexts_b}\n\nRules:\n- Only say same_person=true if there is strong evidence they are the same character (name overlap, title variants, obvious nickname/full-name relationship, or clear contextual cues).\n- If the only overlap is a last name, be VERY cautious (family members/spouses share last names).\n- If they never appear in overlapping chapters, be cautious unless it's clearly a full-name/short-name relationship.\n\nReturn JSON:\n{{\n  \"same_person\": true/false,\n  \"confidence\": 0.0-1.0,\n  \"canonical\": \"Which of the two names should be canonical if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\",\n  \"alias\": \"The other name if same_person=true (must be exactly one of the provided names)\",\n  \"reason\": \"brief justification\"\n}}\"\"\"\n\n\nALIAS_RESOLUTION_SYSTEM = \"\"\"You are a literary analyst identifying character aliases.\n\nYour task is to determine which character names refer to the SAME PERSON.\n\nIMPORTANT: Titles, ranks, and honorifics should be IGNORED when matching names.\nThe underlying PERSON is what matters, not how they're addressed. Examples:\n- Military: \"SSgt Otto\" = \"Staff Sergeant Mark Otto\" = \"Mark Otto\" (but NOT just \"Otto\" alone)\n- Civilian: \"Mr. Smith\" = \"John Smith\" (but NOT just \"Smith\" alone - could be family member)\n- Religious: \"Father O'Brien\" = \"Patrick O'Brien\" (but NOT just \"O'Brien\" alone)\n- Academic: \"Professor Williams\" = \"Dr. Williams\" = \"Jane Williams\"\n\nCRITICAL RULES - BE CONSERVATIVE:\n1. Only merge names if they CLEARLY refer to the same individual with HIGH CONFIDENCE\n2. Different people with similar names should NEVER be merged\n3. Pay close attention to chapter appearances and context clues\n4. When in doubt, keep names SEPARATE - false negatives are better than false positives\n5. A bare FIRST name can merge with a full name (e.g., \"Mike\" → \"Michael Anderson\")\n6. A bare LAST name is RISKY - only merge if you're absolutely certain (could be family member)\n\nFor the canonical name, prefer the FULL CIVILIAN NAME (first + last) over titled versions.\n\nNEVER merge:","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2317}}}
